# linting had been DEAD for months and nothing noticed: TypeScript was upgraded
# to 7.x (the native Go port, which ships no classic `ts.*` compiler API), so
# @typescript-eslint/typescript-estree threw at module load and every eslint run
# aborted before reading a single file. `tsc` still passed, tests still passed.
# A hook makes the linter's absence loud instead of silent.
#
# Staged-scoped for speed: runs only when a workspace TS/TSX file is staged, and
# lints only those files. `--max-warnings` is deliberately NOT set to 0 — there
# are 6 pre-existing advisory warnings (setState-in-effect, array-index keys)
# that are tracked, not blocking. ERRORS fail the commit.
# Bypass in a true emergency with `git commit --no-verify`.
STAGED_TS=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM \
  | grep -E '^(frontend-game/(src|tests)|packages/[^/]+/src)/.*\.(ts|tsx)$' || true)
if [ -n "$STAGED_TS" ]; then
  echo "pre-commit: eslint on $(echo "$STAGED_TS" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') staged file(s)"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  (cd "$ROOT" && npx --no-install eslint --config eslint.config.mjs $STAGED_TS) || {
    echo "pre-commit: eslint FAILED — fix the errors above (or 'npm run lint:fix')." >&2
    exit 1
  }
fi

# T48az -- the GOAL's proof takes nine inputs and its docstring documents four. The
# `PROVEN / ran: 7` in the plan was assembled by hand and never written down, so nothing in
# the repo could reproduce the plan's own central claim. The wrapper owns that derivation.
# Only the selftest runs here; the derivation needs a live stack. What it pins is the
# fail-closed property: the first version validated inside `$(...)`, so `die` exited the
# SUBSHELL and the script sailed on with an empty --project-id, which the proof turned into
# a SKIP. Five of the seven cases are negative, and they drive the REAL function.
bash "$ROOT/scripts/architecture-live-proof-iso.sh" --selftest

# T48ay -- PERF-3's Go leg recognised a page cap only by the NAME of a helper
# (clampLimit/parseLimitOffset), and glossary-service and statistics-service have
# no such helper: every one of their list routes clamps INLINE and read as
# unbounded. Six sat in the lint's own BASELINE with the note "clearing these
# properly means teaching the lint to find the clamp". The inline detector is
# two regexes and a function-scope resolver, each one character wide when broken,
# so the four NEGATIVE cases are what this pins -- notably that the guard assigns
# the SAME variable it tests. A hand-bite of that backreference is invisible here.
"$PY" "$ROOT/scripts/pagination-cap-lint.py" --selftest

# ── The 12-phase workflow gate — UNIVERSAL as of 2026-08-03 ────────────────────
#
# Until today this ran ONLY from `.claude/settings.json`'s PreToolUse hook, so it
# fired when Claude Code issued `git commit` and for nobody else. That was fine
# when Claude was effectively the only agent here. It stopped being fine the day
# this became a five-agent repo: a contributor on Codex, Cursor or Copilot — and
# any human typing `git commit` — walked straight past it, because none of them
# read `.claude/settings.json`.
#
# A check whose scope reaches one of five callers is the "scope never reaches it"
# shape from docs/standards/non-vacuity.md. Moved here, where git enforces it for
# everyone regardless of what wrote the commit.
#
# IT DOES NOT BLOCK A CONTRIBUTOR WHO NEVER STARTED A RUN, and that is the property
# that makes putting it here safe rather than hostile. `workflow-gate.py pre-commit`
# fails OPEN when there is no `.workflow-state.json` — it prints "No workflow state
# found. Proceeding without enforcement." and exits 0. It blocks only someone who
# classified a task and then tried to commit mid-way. Verified through this whole
# chain, both directions: no state -> exit 0; size classified, VERIFY not recorded
# -> exit 1.
#
# That distinction is the whole design. An always-on phase gate would stop a
# first-time contributor on their first commit, and the thing they would reach for
# is `--no-verify`, which switches off db-safety, the provider gate and the secret
# scan along with it. A gate that teaches people to disable the gates is worse than
# no gate.
#
# It is LAST on purpose. The gates above find defects in the code; this one finds
# a missing phase. Running it last means a blocked commit reports BOTH, instead of
# sending you to fix the phase state and only then discovering a real finding.
#
# If it blocks you: record the evidence the phase actually has
# (`python scripts/workflow-gate.py complete verify "<evidence>"`), or state the
# skip (`... skip verify "<reason>"`). Do not reach for --no-verify.
"$PY" "$ROOT/scripts/workflow-gate.py" pre-commit
